1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,599
You tap Buy in a shopping app. A spinner turns for half a second, and then it says: done.

2
00:00:06,149 --> 00:00:10,223
In that half second, at least three different programs just talked to each other.

3
00:00:10,773 --> 00:00:13,114
Today you meet all three: the frontend,

4
00:00:13,114 --> 00:00:13,834
the backend,

5
00:00:13,834 --> 00:00:14,854
and the database.

6
00:00:14,854 --> 00:00:18,155
And then you take a real app apart with your own hands.

7
00:00:18,705 --> 00:00:20,558
Because as a dev ops engineer,

8
00:00:20,558 --> 00:00:21,546
you will deploy,

9
00:00:21,546 --> 00:00:21,916
scale,

10
00:00:21,916 --> 00:00:23,214
and fix these pieces.

11
00:00:23,214 --> 00:00:25,190
So you must know which is which.

12
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,867
Think of a restaurant. The dining room, the kitchen, and the pantry.

13
00:00:31,417 --> 00:00:33,599
The frontend is the dining room.

14
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:36,872
It is what the customer sees and touches: pages,

15
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:37,418
buttons,

16
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:37,827
forms.

17
00:00:37,827 --> 00:00:39,395
It runs in the browser.

18
00:00:39,945 --> 00:00:41,580
The backend is the kitchen.

19
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:42,730
It holds the rules.

20
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:43,941
It checks the order,

21
00:00:43,941 --> 00:00:45,152
works out the price,

22
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:46,665
and decides what happens.

23
00:00:47,215 --> 00:00:49,028
The database is the pantry.

24
00:00:49,028 --> 00:00:49,901
It remembers.

25
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:50,505
Products,

26
00:00:50,505 --> 00:00:50,908
users,

27
00:00:50,908 --> 00:00:51,377
orders,

28
00:00:51,377 --> 00:00:52,586
all stored safely,

29
00:00:52,586 --> 00:00:54,533
even when the power goes off.

30
00:00:55,083 --> 00:00:56,836
They talk in a fixed order.

31
00:00:56,836 --> 00:00:58,719
The browser asks the backend.

32
00:00:58,719 --> 00:01:00,666
The backend asks the database.

33
00:01:00,666 --> 00:01:02,549
Then the answer travels back.

34
00:01:03,099 --> 00:01:08,902
One more word. A monolith is when all of it lives in one program, running as one process.

35
00:01:09,452 --> 00:01:12,623
A service is a smaller program that does one job.

36
00:01:12,623 --> 00:01:13,853
Split the monolith,

37
00:01:13,853 --> 00:01:15,212
and you get services.

38
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:23,171
Let us see a real one. A tiny shop called cloud cart. Go in. Type: CD, cloud cart.

39
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:25,620
Look around. Type: L S.

40
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:32,933
app dot P Y is the whole shop in one file. Start it. Type: dot slash start dot S H, mono.

41
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:35,202
Now act like a browser.

42
00:01:35,202 --> 00:01:36,473
Ask for the page.

43
00:01:36,473 --> 00:01:37,295
Type: curl,

44
00:01:37,295 --> 00:01:37,819
dash S,

45
00:01:37,819 --> 00:01:38,641
local host,

46
00:01:38,641 --> 00:01:39,090
colon,

47
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:40,734
eight zero eight zero,

48
00:01:40,734 --> 00:01:41,108
pipe,

49
00:01:41,108 --> 00:01:41,482
head,

50
00:01:41,482 --> 00:01:42,229
dash four.

51
00:01:42,779 --> 00:01:44,967
That is the frontend: H T M L,

52
00:01:44,967 --> 00:01:46,134
the page itself.

53
00:01:46,134 --> 00:01:47,665
Now ask for the data.

54
00:01:47,665 --> 00:01:48,467
Type: curl,

55
00:01:48,467 --> 00:01:48,978
dash S,

56
00:01:48,978 --> 00:01:53,645
local host colon eight zero eight zero slash API slash products,

57
00:01:53,645 --> 00:01:54,009
pipe,

58
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:54,301
J Q,

59
00:01:54,301 --> 00:01:54,812
dash C,

60
00:01:54,812 --> 00:01:55,249
quote,

61
00:01:55,249 --> 00:01:55,541
dot,

62
00:01:55,541 --> 00:01:56,489
open bracket,

63
00:01:56,489 --> 00:01:57,510
close bracket,

64
00:01:57,510 --> 00:01:57,947
quote.

65
00:01:58,497 --> 00:02:01,380
That is the backend talking: plain data,

66
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:02,676
in jay son format.

67
00:02:02,676 --> 00:02:04,046
J Q only tidies it,

68
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:05,559
one product per line.

69
00:02:06,109 --> 00:02:12,189
Where does the backend get it? From the database. Type: python three, show D B dot P Y.

70
00:02:12,739 --> 00:02:14,599
The same three products,

71
00:02:14,599 --> 00:02:17,468
stored in a file called shop dot D B.

72
00:02:17,468 --> 00:02:19,793
Now count the running servers.

73
00:02:19,793 --> 00:02:20,569
Type: S S,

74
00:02:20,569 --> 00:02:21,421
dash L T N.

75
00:02:21,971 --> 00:02:22,816
One listener,

76
00:02:22,816 --> 00:02:24,571
port eight zero eight zero.

77
00:02:24,571 --> 00:02:27,755
The page and the data come from the same process.

78
00:02:27,755 --> 00:02:28,990
That is a monolith.

79
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:34,347
Now split it. Go in. Type: CD, cloud cart.

80
00:02:34,897 --> 00:02:39,441
Same shop, two services. Type: dot slash start dot S H, split.

81
00:02:39,991 --> 00:02:43,340
What is listening now? Type: S S, dash L T N.

82
00:02:43,890 --> 00:02:44,951
Two listeners.

83
00:02:44,951 --> 00:02:47,527
Port five thousand is the backend.

84
00:02:47,527 --> 00:02:50,785
Port eight zero eight zero is the frontend.

85
00:02:50,785 --> 00:02:52,679
Ask the backend directly.

86
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:53,512
Type: curl,

87
00:02:53,512 --> 00:02:54,042
dash S,

88
00:02:54,042 --> 00:02:58,285
local host colon five thousand slash API slash products,

89
00:02:58,285 --> 00:02:58,664
pipe,

90
00:02:58,664 --> 00:02:58,967
J Q,

91
00:02:58,967 --> 00:02:59,497
dash C,

92
00:02:59,497 --> 00:02:59,952
quote,

93
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:01,846
dot bracket zero bracket,

94
00:03:01,846 --> 00:03:02,301
quote.

95
00:03:02,851 --> 00:03:03,602
Only data.

96
00:03:03,602 --> 00:03:04,204
No page.

97
00:03:04,204 --> 00:03:05,482
Now the frontend.

98
00:03:05,482 --> 00:03:07,962
Which address does its page call?

99
00:03:07,962 --> 00:03:08,789
Type: curl,

100
00:03:08,789 --> 00:03:09,315
dash S,

101
00:03:09,315 --> 00:03:12,247
local host colon eight zero eight zero,

102
00:03:12,247 --> 00:03:12,622
pipe,

103
00:03:12,622 --> 00:03:12,998
grep,

104
00:03:12,998 --> 00:03:14,051
five thousand.

105
00:03:14,601 --> 00:03:15,480
There it is.

106
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:19,585
The page itself calls the backend at port five thousand.

107
00:03:19,585 --> 00:03:20,538
Two programs,

108
00:03:20,538 --> 00:03:21,125
one job.

109
00:03:21,125 --> 00:03:22,224
Stop them both.

110
00:03:22,224 --> 00:03:24,350
Type: dot slash stop dot S H.

111
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:26,585
Clean. Nothing left running.

112
00:03:28,635 --> 00:03:32,774
Now see it the way a customer does. Open the shop in a real browser.

113
00:03:33,324 --> 00:03:38,465
This is the frontend: the page, drawn by the browser, with cards, colors, and prices.

114
00:03:39,015 --> 00:03:41,618
Where did those names and prices come from?

115
00:03:41,618 --> 00:03:43,253
The page asked the backend,

116
00:03:43,253 --> 00:03:45,372
and the backend asked the database.

117
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:48,005
Open the backend's own address,

118
00:03:48,005 --> 00:03:50,021
and there is no design at all.

119
00:03:50,021 --> 00:03:50,962
Just the data,

120
00:03:50,962 --> 00:03:51,701
as jay son.

121
00:03:51,701 --> 00:03:52,440
Same facts,

122
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:53,112
two faces.

123
00:03:53,662 --> 00:03:58,270
The frontend is for people. The backend is for programs. Remember that sentence.

124
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:04,608
So which is better, a monolith or services? Neither. It is a trade.

125
00:04:05,158 --> 00:04:07,092
Services can scale separately.

126
00:04:07,092 --> 00:04:09,155
A busy backend gets more copies,

127
00:04:09,155 --> 00:04:11,025
and the frontend stays small.

128
00:04:11,575 --> 00:04:15,841
They can be deployed separately. Fix a button, and you do not touch the backend.

129
00:04:16,391 --> 00:04:19,165
And one can fail without taking everything down.

130
00:04:19,715 --> 00:04:20,986
But there is a price.

131
00:04:20,986 --> 00:04:21,834
More programs,

132
00:04:21,834 --> 00:04:22,500
more ports,

133
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:23,650
more network calls,

134
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:26,435
and more places to look when something breaks.

135
00:04:26,985 --> 00:04:28,771
So a small team starts simple,

136
00:04:28,771 --> 00:04:30,260
and splits when it hurts.

137
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:32,165
Your job is to know both shapes,

138
00:04:32,165 --> 00:04:33,833
and to see them on a server.

139
00:04:34,383 --> 00:04:39,375
Remember the commands: S S shows what is listening, and curl shows what each one says.

140
00:04:41,425 --> 00:04:44,198
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

141
00:04:44,748 --> 00:04:47,353
The lab is lab sixty-seven dot S H,

142
00:04:47,353 --> 00:04:51,148
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

143
00:04:51,148 --> 00:04:52,636
slash it dash waves,

144
00:04:52,636 --> 00:04:54,348
slash dev ops dash A I.

145
00:04:54,348 --> 00:04:56,059
Install it like before,

146
00:04:56,059 --> 00:04:57,250
then run lab up.

147
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:02,119
It starts cloud cart as one monolith on port eight zero eight zero.

148
00:05:02,119 --> 00:05:03,666
Your job is to split it.

149
00:05:04,216 --> 00:05:08,888
One: start the backend alone, so port five thousand answers with the product list.

150
00:05:09,438 --> 00:05:14,537
Two: make the frontend serve the page on port eight zero eight zero, and call the backend.

151
00:05:15,087 --> 00:05:17,875
Three: the two must be separate processes.

152
00:05:17,875 --> 00:05:21,060
Read start dot S H; the answer is already there.

153
00:05:21,610 --> 00:05:23,361
Four: in answers dot text,

154
00:05:23,361 --> 00:05:24,708
write one line: D B,

155
00:05:24,708 --> 00:05:25,112
colon,

156
00:05:25,112 --> 00:05:28,074
then the service that talks to the database.

157
00:05:28,624 --> 00:05:31,120
Then run lab check. Four pass lines.

158
00:05:33,170 --> 00:05:35,154
Six points, and you need all six.

159
00:05:35,704 --> 00:05:37,539
The checker shows four pass lines.

160
00:05:38,089 --> 00:05:40,158
You can show the backend alone with curl.

161
00:05:40,708 --> 00:05:43,396
You can show who listens on which port with S S.

162
00:05:43,946 --> 00:05:48,234
You can explain frontend, backend, and database in one sentence each.

163
00:05:48,784 --> 00:05:51,813
You can explain one benefit and one cost of splitting.

164
00:05:52,363 --> 00:05:55,094
You ran lab down, and nothing is left listening.

165
00:05:55,644 --> 00:05:57,265
To pass, you need all six.

166
00:05:59,315 --> 00:06:02,152
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

167
00:06:02,702 --> 00:06:04,921
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

168
00:06:05,471 --> 00:06:08,842
Two: run S S dash L T N, and find the monolith.

169
00:06:09,392 --> 00:06:11,440
Three: split it, with the start script.

170
00:06:11,990 --> 00:06:17,536
Four: curl the backend on port five thousand, and the page on port eight zero eight zero.

171
00:06:18,086 --> 00:06:21,180
Five: write answers dot text, then run lab check.

172
00:06:21,730 --> 00:06:23,202
Six: run lab down.

173
00:06:23,752 --> 00:06:25,586
Pause the video, and begin now.

174
00:06:41,136 --> 00:06:42,011
Quick check.

175
00:06:42,561 --> 00:06:44,907
What does the frontend do, and where does it run?

176
00:06:45,457 --> 00:06:48,081
What does the backend do that the frontend should not?

177
00:06:48,631 --> 00:06:50,637
Why does a database exist at all?

178
00:06:51,187 --> 00:06:54,472
Name one benefit and one cost of splitting a monolith.

179
00:06:55,022 --> 00:06:57,595
Objective covered: M C zero eight,

180
00:06:57,595 --> 00:06:58,655
objective one,

181
00:06:58,655 --> 00:06:59,336
frontend,

182
00:06:59,336 --> 00:06:59,941
backend,

183
00:06:59,941 --> 00:07:00,622
monolith,

184
00:07:00,622 --> 00:07:01,228
service,

185
00:07:01,228 --> 00:07:02,211
and database.

186
00:07:02,761 --> 00:07:04,831
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

187
00:07:15,381 --> 00:07:19,306
Next: requests, responses, rest, status codes, and jay son.

